A mobile app is just software the operating system launches inside a *sandbox* — an isolated space with limited, permission-gated access to the device. There are three broad ways to build one. *Native* apps are written against a single platform's toolkit (Swift/SwiftUI on iOS, Kotlin/Jetpack Compose on Android); they get full performance and the newest device features first. *Cross-platform* frameworks such as React Native or Flutter let you write mostly one codebase and run it on both platforms, trading a little rawness for a lot of shared work. A *PWA* (Progressive Web App) is a website that adds a *manifest* (so it can be installed to the home screen) and a *service worker* (so it can cache files and work offline) — no app store required. Here is the same tiny piece of UI expressed in a cross-platform framework:
```jsx
// React Native: one component, renders on iOS and Android
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <Text>Hello, {name}!</Text>;
}
```
The framework maps `<Text>` onto each platform's real text control at runtime.

*State* is the data your UI reflects right now — a toggle, a form, a loaded list. It lives in memory and vanishes when the app is closed, so anything that must survive goes into *persistent storage*: a simple *key-value* store for small things (a theme, a login token) or a local *database* (like SQLite) for structured records and offline use. Fresh or shared data comes from a *backend* over the network: the app makes an *HTTP request* to an *API endpoint* and usually gets *JSON* back. Because networks are slow and fail, every request has three UI states — loading, success, and error:
```js
async function loadUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Request failed');
  return res.json(); // parsed into an object the UI can render
}
```
A robust screen shows a spinner while awaiting, the data on success, and a retry option on error.

The sandbox blocks sensitive hardware until the user grants *permission*. The *camera* captures photos or video (and feeds AR, scanning, and vision features). *Location* comes from GPS plus Wi-Fi and cell signals, offered at coarse or precise granularity — you request only what you need. *Notifications* come in two flavors: *local* notifications the app schedules itself (a reminder) and *push* notifications sent from a server through the platform's push service to reach a device even when the app is closed. The pattern is always the same: check the permission, request it *in context* (explain why, right when the feature is used), and handle denial without breaking:
```js
const status = await requestPermission('camera');
if (status !== 'granted') {
  showMessage('You can enable the camera later in Settings.');
  return; // fall back — never crash on a 'no'
}
openCamera();
```
